<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Algerian War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">ended <a href="/years/1962">1962</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Algerian War was the war of independence fought by Algerian nationalists, led by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-liberation-front-south-yemen">National Liberation Front</a>, against French rule from 1954 until it ended in 1962. It involved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> warfare, urban terrorism, counter-insurgency and widespread use of torture. It caused a political crisis in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and ended with Algerian independence, a major blow to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-colonial-empire">French Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/algerian-war.jpg" alt="Algerian War" width="320" height="237" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zdravko Pečar · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:National_Liberation_Army_Soldiers_(7).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a> in June 1830, on the pretext of a slight to its consul, after Charles X and his ministers decided in January of that year on an invasion. The conquest spread inland under Marshal Bugeaud, the first Governor-General, and was violent, marked by a scorched earth policy. Between 500,000 and 1,000,000 of about 3 million Algerians are estimated to have been killed in the first three decades. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> was declared an integral part of France by the Constitution of 1848, in three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a>, and French and other Europeans settled there; they later became known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pieds-noirs">pieds-noirs</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The war effectively began on 1 November 1954, the Toussaint Rouge, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrillas</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-liberation-front-south-yemen">National Liberation Front</a> (FLN) attacked military and civilian targets across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>. The FLN was weak at first, and its armed wing had only some 3,000 badly equipped men. According to the historian Natalya Vince, its leaders knew they could not win militarily and used guerrilla warfare and urban terrorism to erode <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>'s political will and expose French repression.</p><p>They also worked to internationalise the conflict, which became entangled with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a>. French forces used torture, indiscriminate shootings and bombing of villages, destroyed over 8,000 villages and moved over 2 million Algerians into regroupment camps. More than 12,000 Algerians died in FLN purges. Reports of torture reaching <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1956 and 1957 made it known there as la sale guerre, the dirty war.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The war brought down the Fourth Republic in 1958, replaced by the Fifth with a stronger presidency. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-de-gaulle">Charles de Gaulle</a> opened negotiations with the FLN, which ended in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/evian-accords">Évian Accords</a> of March 1962. French voters approved them on 8 April 1962 with 91 percent, and on 1 July 99.72 percent in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> voted for independence. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/organisation-armee-secrete">Organisation armée secrète</a>, which opposed withdrawal, carried out bombings and murders and tried to assassinate de Gaulle.</p><p>Estimates of Algerian deaths range from 400,000 to 1.5 million, with 25,600 French soldiers and 6,000 Europeans also killed. In a few months of 1962, 900,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pieds-noirs">pieds-noirs</a> fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Harkis, Algerians who had served as auxiliaries with the French army, were regarded as traitors and many were murdered; about 20,000 Harki families reached France.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Algerian War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A member of the Eastern Bloc, North Vietnam was recognised by almost all communist countries, China first, and received their aid; many non-aligned states gave it de facto recognition, and its clandestine weapon transfers during the <b>Algerian War</b> made relations with Algeria close.</blockquote><a href="/empire/democratic-republic-of-vietnam">Democratic Republic of Vietnam · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Algerian War</b> that followed cost an estimated 300,000 to 1.5 million Algerian lives across eight years.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-empire">French Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>De Gaulle ended the <b>Algerian War</b> with Algerian independence in 1962, introduced direct election of the president that year, built an independent nuclear deterrent and left NATO's integrated command in 1966.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-fifth-republic">French Fifth Republic</a></li></ul></section>
